\subsection{Axis-dependent regularization}
\label{sec:adaptation-rs-anisotropy}
Applying the scalar objective to axis $i$ gives the diagnostic ratio
\begin{equation}
\rho_i^\star=\frac{r_{S,i}^\star}{r_{S,z}^\star}
=\left(\frac{q_i}{q_z}\right)^{1/14}
 \left(\frac{m_{-4,i}}{m_{-4,z}}\right)^{3/7},\qquad i=x,y.
\label{eq:adapt-rs-axis-ratios}
\end{equation}
Desired-motion spectra therefore matter more logarithmically than drift
intensity alone. The implemented empirical factors are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct r_S&=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}[\rho_x,\rho_y,1]^\top,\\
\mat R_S&=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^2\diag(\rho_x^2,\rho_y^2,1).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}
\end{equation}
Here $\rho_x=0.72$, $\rho_y=0.50$, and $\rho_z=1.0$ multiply standard deviations, not covariance entries. The stationary OU
prior remains isotropic, with stationary standard-deviation matrix $\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}\mat I_3$.
These regularizer factors are fixed in world NED and are not rotated with heading;
zero-yaw reference records align the horizontal axes with surge and sway.
They are neither the exact scalar optima nor a heading-invariant vessel model.
